# usePresence

`usePresence` and `useIsPresent` let a component branch on whether
`<AnimatePresence>` is keeping it alive for an exit phase. The wrapper
(`<PresenceChild>`) holds the child rendered while `isPresent` is `false`,
and the child runs its own exit animation — CSS transition, canvas effect,
GSAP, anything — and calls `safeToRemove()` when finished.

This is the path to take when the built-in `motion.*` `exit` prop isn't
enough — for example, animating a third-party component, fading text via
CSS classes, or coordinating an exit with non-DOM work.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { AnimatePresence, PresenceChild, usePresence } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    let visible = $state(true)
</script>

<button onclick={() => (visible = !visible)}>Toggle</button>

<AnimatePresence>
    <PresenceChild present={visible}>
        <Card />
    </PresenceChild>
</AnimatePresence>
```

Inside `<Card>`, call `usePresence()` once in the component's `<script>`, then read
`presence` in `$effect` or the template. Destructuring it at the top level of
`<script>` reads it only once.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { usePresence } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    const presence = usePresence()
    const isPresent = $derived(presence[0])
    let node: HTMLElement | undefined = $state()

    $effect(() => {
        const [present, safeToRemove] = presence
        if (present || !node || !safeToRemove) return
        const el = node
        const onEnd = (e: TransitionEvent) => {
            if (e.target !== el) return
            safeToRemove()
        }
        el.addEventListener('transitionend', onEnd, { once: true })
        return () => el.removeEventListener('transitionend', onEnd)
    })
</script>

<div bind:this={node} class="card" class:exiting={!isPresent}>…</div>

<style>
    .card { transition: opacity 300ms, transform 300ms; }
    .card.exiting { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-12px); }
</style>
```

<Example isSmall exampleUrl="/examples/use-presence">
  <UsePresenceExample />
</Example>

## API divergence from React

In framer-motion, `usePresence` works directly inside `<AnimatePresence>` —
React's render tree gives the library control over when children unmount.
Svelte's `{#if}` teardown is synchronous from the user's side and not
interceptable, so you opt-in via the `<PresenceChild>` wrapper. Bind
`present` to the same condition that would normally gate the children:

```svelte
<!-- React (framer-motion) -->
<AnimatePresence>
    {visible && <Card />}
</AnimatePresence>

<!-- Svelte equivalent -->
<AnimatePresence>
    <PresenceChild present={visible}>
        <Card />
    </PresenceChild>
</AnimatePresence>
```

## `useIsPresent`

Returns just the `isPresent` boolean. Useful when you only need to render
different content during exit, no `safeToRemove` needed:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { useIsPresent } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'
    const isPresent = $derived(useIsPresent())
</script>

<div data-state={isPresent ? 'live' : 'exiting'}>…</div>
```

When called outside any `PresenceChild`, `useIsPresent()` returns `true` and
`usePresence()` returns `[true, null]`. `useIsPresent()` never registers with
the wrapper, so it never holds an exit.

## How `safeToRemove` behaves

- **Idempotent.** Calling it twice is a no-op after the first.
- **Versioned.** Re-entering (`present` flipping back to `true`) before
  `safeToRemove` fires cancels the exit; the previously-handed-out callback
  becomes a no-op so a stale `transitionend` handler can't tear down a
  now-present component.
- **Per participant.** Each `usePresence()` consumer is one participant of its
  wrapper, and its `safeToRemove` completes only that participant. The wrapper
  (or owned `child` snippet) leaves when every participant is done.

## Mixing with `motion.*` `exit`

Inside `<PresenceChild>`, independent `motion.*` descendants run their `exit`
animations on the real nodes. The wrapper automatically waits until **all**
registered motion exits finish, then removes the whole subtree and reports one
completion to `<AnimatePresence>`. The same coordination applies to an owned
`child()` snippet containing a plain wrapper with multiple motion descendants.

As in Motion, every `usePresence()` consumer is a participant too. Its
`safeToRemove()` completes only that consumer, so it never cuts a slower motion
exit short: the wrapper leaves when every participant (motion exits, layout
measurement, other `usePresence()` consumers) is done. A wrapper with nothing
registered (plain elements, or an `m` without `LazyMotion` features) leaves as
soon as its exit starts.

Re-entering cancels the current automatic exit cycle. Stale animation
completions cannot finish a newer exit. Removing an exiting motion descendant
also removes its pending participation, so it cannot leave the wrapper stuck.

See [the owned group example](/examples/use-presence) for fast and slow exits
that retain their common wrapper until both finish.

## Migrating from `$derived(usePresence())`

Earlier versions asked you to re-call `usePresence()` inside `$derived`. That
pattern still works but is deprecated: a reactive call can't register with the
wrapper, so the wrapper keeps the old rules. It doesn't leave on its own when
nothing else is registered, and that call's `safeToRemove` releases the whole
wrapper, motion descendants included. Development builds warn once per wrapper.

```svelte
<!-- Before (deprecated) -->
<script lang="ts">
    const presence = $derived(usePresence())
</script>

<!-- After -->
<script lang="ts">
    const presence = usePresence()
</script>
```

The rest of the component (`$derived(presence[0])`, destructuring inside
`$effect`) stays the same.

## Known limitations

- **Nested `<AnimatePresence>` inside a held `<PresenceChild>`**: while
  the wrapper is holding, descendants don't see exit signals because the
  Svelte tree is still mounted. Once the wrapper leaves, normal unmount
  fires, and any nested motion children's `exit` runs at that point.

## API Reference

### `<PresenceChild>` props

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
| ---- | ---- | ------- | ----------- |
| `present` | `boolean` | `true` | When this flips `true → false`, the wrapper holds children rendered with `isPresent=false` until every registered participant is done (with nothing registered, at once). |
| `children` | `Snippet` | — | Snippet rendered while `present` is true or while the wrapper is holding. |

### `useIsPresent(): boolean`

Returns whether the calling component is currently present. `true` outside
of any `<PresenceChild>`.

### `usePresence(): [true, null] | [false, () => void]`

Registers the calling component with its wrapper at init and returns a
reactive two-item array in the framer-motion tuple shape: `[true, null]` while
present (or outside any `<PresenceChild>`); `[false, () => void]` once the
wrapper enters its exit hold. The callback completes only this consumer.

## See also

- [AnimatePresence](/docs/animate-presence) — the parent component.
- [`motion.*` `exit` prop](https://motion.dev/docs/react-motion-component#exit) —
  declarative alternative when a built-in transform/style animation is enough.

---

Based on [Motion's usePresence](https://motion.dev/docs/react-use-presence) API.
